// Prepare, entangle, measure ancilla, then decode.","filename":"surface_code_round.qasm","framework":"OpenQASM 3.0","language":"openqasm","note":"The reference keeps syndrome and decoder boundaries visible rather than pretending a universal hardware layout.","status":"native"}],"description":"A fault-tolerance record for comparing physical error, syndrome extraction, decoder choice, and logical failure.","descriptionJa":"物理誤り、シンドローム抽出、デコーダ、論理故障を比較するためのフォールトトレランス項目です。","explanation":"Repeated stabilizer measurements produce a space-time syndrome. A decoder infers likely error chains, and the logical observable fails when the inferred correction differs by a nontrivial logical operator.","explanationJa":"反復シンドローム測定が時空間シンドロームを作り、デコーダがエラー鎖を推定します。推定回復が非自明な論理演算子と異なると論理観測量が失敗します。","explanationMd":"Surface codes protect a logical qubit using only local stabilizer checks on a 2D lattice: data qubits sit on edges or vertices, and repeated measurement of local $X$-type and $Z$-type stabilizer operators produces a syndrome without ever measuring the encoded logical state directly. Stacking syndrome rounds over time traces out a space-time syndrome graph; a decoder (typically minimum-weight perfect matching or a neural/tensor-network decoder) infers the most likely error chain consistent with that graph, and the logical observable fails only when the true error and the inferred correction differ by a nontrivial logical operator — an event whose rate drops super-linearly below the physical error rate once the code operates below its threshold. The XZZX variant this record cites reshapes the stabilizers so that a biased (e.g. dephasing-dominated) physical noise channel is matched more efficiently than the standard surface code, without needing a higher weight. This record documents the syndrome-round and logical-observable workflow as an architecture reference; it makes no hardware threshold claim of its own, and — per its caveat — decoder quality, leakage, measurement error, and connectivity determine the useful operating point.","explanationMdJa":"表面符号は、2次元格子上の局所的なスタビライザー検査だけで論理量子ビットを保護します。データ量子ビットは辺または頂点に配置され、局所的な$X$型・$Z$型スタビライザー演算子を反復測定することで、符号化された論理状態を直接測ることなくシンドロームが得られます。時間方向にシンドロームラウンドを重ねると時空間シンドロームグラフが得られ、デコーダ(典型的には最小重み完全マッチングやニューラル・テンソルネットワーク型デコーダ)がそのグラフと整合する最も尤もらしいエラー鎖を推定します。論理観測量が失敗するのは、真の誤りと推定した補正が非自明な論理演算子だけ異なる場合であり、符号が閾値以下で動作していれば、符号距離を大きくするにつれて論理誤り率を急速に下げられます。この項目が引用するXZZX変種は、標準的な表面符号よりも重みを増やさずに偏った(例えば位相減衰が支配的な)物理ノイズチャネルに効率よく適合するようスタビライザーを再構成したものです。この項目はシンドロームラウンドと論理観測量のワークフローをアーキテクチャ参照として記録しており、それ自体はハードウェア閾値を主張せず、注記の通りデコーダの質、リーケージ、測定誤り、接続性が実用上の動作点を決めます。","exportStatus":"OpenQASM-oriented stabilizer checks · hardware layout not universal","framework":"OpenQASM 3.0","industryUseCases":["Fault-tolerant quantum processor design","Decoder co-design and calibration","Logical qubit memory experiments"],"industryUseCasesJa":["フォールトトレラント量子プロセッサ設計","デコーダとキャリブレーションの協調設計","論理量子ビットメモリ実験"],"introduction":"Surface-code families are central to scalable fault tolerance because they use local stabilizer checks and planar connectivity. The XZZX variant illustrates why matching a code to biased noise can matter as much as the code name.","introductionJa":"表面符号系は局所シンドローム検査と平面接続を使うため、スケーラブルなフォールトトレランスの中心です。XZZXはノイズの偏りに符号を合わせる重要性を示します。","literature":[{"authors":"J. Pablo Bonilla Ataides, David K. Tuckett, Stephen D. Bartlett, Steven T. Flammia, Benjamin J. Brown","relevance":"Shows how a surface-code variant can exploit structure in biased Pauli noise and decoder design.","relevanceJa":"偏ったパウリノイズとデコーダ設計の構造を活用する表面符号の変種を示します。","title":"The XZZX Surface Code","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2009.07851","year":"2020"},{"authors":"Peter W. Shor","relevance":"Provides a foundational contrast between early block-code protection and scalable local-check architectures.","relevanceJa":"初期のブロック符号とスケーラブルな局所検査構成を比較する基礎文献です。","title":"Scheme for reducing decoherence in quantum computer memory","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/physreva.52.r2493","year":"1995"}],"metadata":[{"label":"Physical layer","value":"Local stabilizers"},{"label":"Noise","value":"Pauli / biased"},{"label":"Output","value":"Logical failure"}],"provenance":"Literature reference","relatedSlugs":["shor-code-error-correction","quantum-teleportation","bell-state-qiskit"],"resources":[{"label":"Signal","value":"Syndrome rounds"},{"label":"Decoder","value":"Noise-aware"},{"label":"Metric","value":"Logical error rate"}],"slug":"surface-code-memory","source":{"kind":"curated_reference","license":"Public bibliographic link; code is an original reference sketch","reviewedBy":"LeonaQ literature curation pass","title":"The XZZX Surface Code","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2009.07851"},"sourceCoverage":{"hardware":"unknown","simulation":"unknown","theory":"unknown"},"status":"community_review","tags":["surface code","fault tolerance","biased noise","decoder"],"title":"Surface-code memory and biased-noise decoding","titleJa":"表面符号メモリと偏りノイズデコーディング","topics":["operator","stabilizer","error-correction"],"updatedAt":"2026-07-14","verification":"Stabilizer-check workflow documented · threshold is noise-model dependent","verificationDetails":{"caveat":"Decoder, leakage, measurement errors, connectivity, and bias determine the useful operating point.","method":"Syndrome rounds and logical-observable framing are documented for a small surface-code-style memory.","result":"Reviewable architecture reference · no hardware threshold claim."},"verificationMethods":["community_submission","construction","research_paper"],"visualization":{"operations":[{"label":"Prepare","qubits":[0],"tone":"accent"},{"label":"Syndrome×d","qubits":[0,1],"tone":"warn"},{"label":"Decode","qubits":[1,2],"tone":"ok"}],"outcomes":[{"label":"Logical pass","probability":0.97}],"wires":["data patch","XZZX checks","decoder"]}}

